<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lei Áurea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Golden Law</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lei Aurea, or Golden Law, was the law of 1888 that abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>. It was signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-princess-imperial-of-brazil">Princess Isabel</a> while acting as regent for her father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a>. Abolition without compensation alienated many slaveholding landowners from the monarchy, contributing to the overthrow of the empire in 1889.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lei-aurea.jpg" alt="Lei Áurea" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Senado Imperial · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lei_%C3%81urea_(Golden_Law).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>For over three centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s economy relied on enslaved labour; of the 10 million African slaves forcibly brought to the New World, 40 percent were taken to Brazil, and the enslaved population grew from about 1 million in 1822 to some 2 to 2.5 million by the mid-19th century. The British-Brazilian Treaty of 1826 made the import of slaves illegal by 1830, and the British Navy seized slave ships. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> came to believe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> could no longer be justified but should end gradually, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> sharpened his fears of internal conflict.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a> (1864–1870) turned many officers, who had fought beside enlisted slaves, against the institution. Some 230 abolitionist organisations were active in the last decades of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, while the Rio Branco Law of 28 September 1871, which freed children born to enslaved parents, and the Saraiva-Cotegipe Law of 28 September 1885, which freed slaves at 60, limited the supply of labour, and landowners turned to European immigration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Officially Law No. 3,353 of 13 May 1888, it had two articles: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was declared extinct in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> from the date of the law, and all provisions to the contrary were revoked. Its brevity was meant to show that the freedom it granted carried no conditions. It was written by the Minister of Agriculture, Rodrigo A. da Silva, in the cabinet of João Alfredo Correia de Oliveira, passed both houses of the General Assembly and was countersigned by da Silva. Pope Leo XIII awarded Isabel the Golden Rose. Brazil was the last country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to abolish slavery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The law provided no support for either side of the change. Former owners received no indemnity, and the freed received no compensation or help from the state; having been barred from owning property or receiving an education, they were left without education or political representation and struggled to gain economic and social standing. Plantation owners had to find workers elsewhere. The uproar among slave owners and the upper classes ended in the fall of the monarchy and the establishment of a republic in 1889, and the law is often regarded as the most immediate, though not the only, cause.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lei Áurea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The abolition of slavery by the <b>Lei Áurea</b> (Golden Law) of 1888, long overdue on moral grounds, freed the remaining enslaved people without compensation to their owners or any provision for the freedpeople themselves.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Brazil’s <b>Golden Law</b> of 13 May 1888, which ended slavery in the last country of the Americas to keep it, has just two articles: the first declares slavery extinct in Brazil, the second revokes all provisions to the contrary.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
